# Ponytail efficiency

- Understand the request and inspect the code path before changing it.
- Prefer YAGNI, existing project code, platform features, the standard library,
  and already-installed dependencies, in that order.
- Choose the smallest correct implementation.
- Delete unnecessary code instead of adding abstractions.
- Do not add dependencies unless they are genuinely required.
- Keep solutions boring, focused, and proportionate to the task.
- Never remove or skip security, validation, authorization, tenancy checks,
  accessibility, error handling, or data-loss prevention.
- For SSCRE, prefer a measured vertical slice over speculative infrastructure.
- Before finalizing substantive Python changes, ensure declared development
  dependencies are installed and run the full test suite when the environment
  permits; report any skipped or unavailable verification explicitly.
- On Windows PowerShell, chain shell commands with `;`, not `&&` (unless the
  shell explicitly supports `&&`).

## Git hygiene (when the user requests commits)

- Propose an ordered commit plan first: purpose, file paths, and message per
  commit. Run `git commit` only after the user confirms, unless they already
  approved that exact plan in the same request.
- Prefer one milestone slice or one logical change per commit. Do not bundle
  multiple milestones in a single commit without stating the tradeoff and
  getting explicit acceptance.
- Keep planning-document commits separate from code commits unless the user
  asks to combine them.
- Review `git status` before staging; avoid blind `git add .` when unrelated
  files are present.
- Run the relevant tests before each commit and report pass, fail, or skip.
